    time you visit.
  - [1mOS notifications (Notification API)[0m — a real system toast. Needs a permission prompt, and still [1monly fires while the
[0m    [1mpage is open[0m unless you add a service worker. Modest cost, better presence.
  - [1mService worker + installed PWA[0m — the only browser-local path that can fire with the tab closed, and even then
    background-sync scheduling is Chromium-only and unreliable. Significant complexity for a guarantee you don't
    actually get.

  If "remind me when I'm not looking" is the actual requirement, none of these deliver it properly and a small server
  would — worth knowing before we commit.

  My recommendation: [1min-page only[0m, with OS notifications as a thin layer on top later if you want them. It's the honest
  version, it's cheap, and it doesn't pretend to a reliability the platform can't provide.

